 ## Subtopics
 
-### [CP Decomposition](2255-cp-decomposition.md)
-CP Decomposition factorizes a tensor into a sum of rank-one components (outer products of vectors). It offers higher compactness than Tucker Decomposition but requires more components for complex scenes, leading to higher reconstruction costs. Choose when memory footprint is the primary constraint and scene complexity allows for higher rank.
-
-### [Tucker Decomposition (HOSVD)](5352-tucker-decomposition-hosvd.md)
-Tucker Decomposition generalizes SVD to tensors, producing a core tensor and factor matrices. It preserves multilinear structure and orthonormality, making it suitable for global basis generation and multiresolution rendering. Choose when preserving structural integrity and enabling multiresolution access is more important than maximum compactness.
-
-### [Karhunen-Loeve Transform (KLT)](5365-karhunen-loeve-transform-klt.md)
-KLT is a linear decorrelation technique using data-dependent eigenvectors of the covariance matrix. It provides superior decorrelation compared to DCT but lacks fast transforms, shifting computation to offline preprocessing. Choose when offline preprocessing cost is acceptable and optimal decorrelation is required.
-
-### [Sparse Representation (Dictionary Based)](5366-sparse-representation-dictionary-based.md)
-Encodes blocks as sparse linear combinations of dictionary atoms. It differs from Vector Quantization by allowing multiple atoms per block, offering higher fidelity at higher encoding complexity. K-SVD is the primary method for learning such dictionaries.
-
 ### Orthogonal Matching Pursuit (OMP)
-A greedy algorithm for sparse coding that selects dictionary atoms sequentially to find an approximate sparse solution $\mathbf{x}$ for $\min \|\mathbf{x}\|_0 \text{ s.t. } \|\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{D}\mathbf{x}\|_2 \leq \epsilon$ [S2, unofficial]. It computes inner products between the signal $\mathbf{y}$ and dictionary columns $\mathbf{D}$, then deploys least squares solvers to update coefficients [S2, unofficial]. The target sparsity $L$ limits the maximum nonzero entries in $\mathbf{x}$ [S2, unofficial]. OMP is preferred over Matching Pursuit (MP) due to improved orthogonality handling and faster convergence [S2, unofficial].
+A greedy algorithm for sparse coding that selects dictionary atoms sequentially to find an approximate sparse solution [S2, unofficial]. It computes inner products between the signal and dictionary columns, then updates coefficients using least squares solvers [S2, unofficial]. It is preferred over Matching Pursuit (MP) due to improved orthogonality and faster convergence [S2, unofficial]. The parameter $L$ defines the maximum number of non-zero entries [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### Compressive Volume Rendering with Dictionary Learning
-A volume rendering technique that treats rendered images as collections of square pixel blocks to apply sparse coding and dictionary learning for recovery or compression [S2, unofficial]. It trains dictionaries on grayscale images first, then extends to color images by linearizing $16 \times 16 \times 3$ blocks [S2, unofficial]. Use when recovering missing pixels or compressing rendered images is the goal [S2, unofficial].
+A technique that treats rendered images as collections of square pixel blocks to apply sparse coding and dictionary learning for recovery or compression [S2, unofficial]. It typically trains a grayscale dictionary first, then extends to RGB by training on 3D blocks ($16 \times 16 \times 3$) to capture inter-channel correlations [S2, unofficial]. The block size is typically $16 \times 16$ pixels [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** Dictionary training is iterative and expensive [G]. Training a $256 \times 1024$ dictionary on grayscale images took 7.1 minutes on a single-core CPU [S2, unofficial]. RGB training took 47.3 minutes [S2, unofficial]. Encoding is dominated by OMP, which is linear in dictionary size per block [G].
-**Memory Complexity:** Dictionary size $D$ (e.g., $1024$ atoms of dimension $256$) is stored in memory [S2, unofficial]. Coefficients are sparse [G].
-**Performance:** Achieves very low bits-per-sample (bps): 0.25 bps for Chameleon, 0.07 bps for Supernova, 0.20 bps for Turbulence [S1, unofficial]. SSIM scores (with deblocking) range from 0.72 to 0.84 [S1, unofficial].
-**Strengths:** High compression ratios; superior reconstruction quality (PSNR) compared to DCT dictionaries, especially with missing data (31.2 dB vs 22.97 dB PSNR with 70% missing pixels) [S2, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** High training time [S2, unofficial]; encoding is slower than fixed-basis methods [G]; sensitive to training data distribution [G].
-**When to use:** Real-time exploration of time-varying datasets during animation playback with pre-loaded compressed data in GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
-**When not to use:** When encoding latency is critical or training data is unavailable [G].
+**Time Complexity:** The training time is dominated by the iterative sparse coding and dictionary update steps. For a $16 \times 16$ grayscale dictionary of size $256 \times 1024$, training took 7.1 minutes on a single-threaded Intel Core i7-4770K CPU [S2, unofficial]. RGB training ($768 \times 2304$) took 47.3 minutes [S2, unofficial].
+**Memory Complexity:** The dictionary size is $M \times N$. For grayscale, $256 \times 1024$ floats; for RGB, $768 \times 2304$ floats [S2, unofficial].
+**Performance:** Achieves bits-per-sample (bps) of 0.25 for Chameleon ($1024^2$), 0.07 for Supernova (1080p), and 0.20 for Turbulence ($512^4$) [S1, unofficial]. Deblocked SSIM scores are 0.72 (Chameleon), 0.76 (Supernova), and 0.84 (Turbulence) [S1, unofficial].
+**Strengths:** Outperforms prescribed DCT dictionaries in reconstruction quality (PSNR) for missing pixel recovery and compression [S2, unofficial]. Captures complex structures better than fixed bases.
+**Limitations:** High training time. Requires a representative training dataset. Performance depends on the choice of sparsity level $L$ and dictionary size [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from dictionary learning techniques in signal processing, adapted for volume rendering by treating rendered images as collections of blocks [S2, unofficial]. The K-SVD algorithm was introduced by Elad and Aharon [MAB06] as a generalization of K-Means [S2, unofficial]. In volume rendering, it was applied to compress and recover rendered images, demonstrating advantages over prescribed bases like DCT [S2, unofficial].
+The K-SVD algorithm was introduced by Elad and Aharon [MAB06] as a generalization of K-Means for dictionary learning. It was applied to volume rendering by [S2, unofficial] to compress and recover volume rendered images, treating them as collections of pixel blocks. The method builds on the principles of sparse representation and dictionary learning [S2, unofficial].
 
